WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.482
Picture a night in a village
in Uttar Pradesh.

2
00:00:03.482 --> 00:00:09.256
An old man is in pain, and his daughter-in-law
turns to the local health worker

3
00:00:09.256 --> 00:00:11.083
with a question in Hindi.

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.163
Family caregiver: His pain
is worse at night… What should we do?

5
00:00:16.887 --> 00:00:20.648
His pain is worse
at night. What should we do?

6
00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:26.757
Palli Sahayak is an open-source voice
assistant for palliative care. Patients,

7
00:00:26.757 --> 00:00:28.912
family caregivers, and ASHA workers,

8
00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:33.642
India's community health workers under the
National Health Mission, can ask out

9
00:00:33.642 --> 00:00:36.216
loud, in their own
language, by phone call,

10
00:00:36.216 --> 00:00:37.474
WhatsApp, or the web.

11
00:00:38.687 --> 00:00:40.344
Why look things up first?

12
00:00:40.344 --> 00:00:45.448
A model answering from memory can
sound sure and still be wrong. In medicine,

13
00:00:45.448 --> 00:00:46.575
that's dangerous.

14
00:00:46.987 --> 00:00:51.829
It searches trusted care guidelines for the
passages closest in meaning to the

15
00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:53.754
question, and keeps only those.

16
00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:59.851
Then a language model turns them into
a short, warm reply, in the caller's language.

17
00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:04.489
The facts come from the guidelines; the
model makes them easy to hear.

18
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.941
Here's a short excerpt from a completely
fictional conversation: made up,

19
00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:11.152
with no real people.

20
00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:17.933
Here, a family caregiver
in rural Uttar Pradesh, in Hindi.

21
00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:21.025
Family caregiver: Hello… namaste.

22
00:01:21.025 --> 00:01:26.324
My father-in-law has cancer…
At night he groans a lot with the pain.

23
00:01:26.324 --> 00:01:30.454
I just sent the kids
to school, so I thought I'd ask.

24
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.887
Palli Sahayak: Namaste, good that you called.
Has the doctor given pain medicine?

25
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.720
Family caregiver: Yes… there's medicine.

26
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.567
But… people in the village say too much
becomes an addiction, so we give only half.

27
00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:52.338
Palli Sahayak: Give the medicine
just as the doctor wrote it;

28
00:01:52.338 --> 00:01:56.743
don't change the amount yourself. If
you're worried about addiction,

29
00:01:56.743 --> 00:01:57.974
talk to the doctor.

30
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:03.061
Family caregiver: Okay…
alright. And food— he hardly eats,

31
00:02:03.061 --> 00:02:07.485
just a few spoons of
porridge… should I make him eat?

32
00:02:08.453 --> 00:02:13.416
Palli Sahayak: No forcing,
just a little of whatever he likes.

33
00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:19.899
Talk to your ASHA didi or doctor today, and
if the pain suddenly gets much worse,

34
00:02:19.899 --> 00:02:21.500
call 108 right away.

35
00:02:24.487 --> 00:02:28.643
Notice the guard rails. It
never gives specific medicine doses.

36
00:02:28.643 --> 00:02:33.922
If it hears danger signs, it tells
the caller to call one zero eight right away,

37
00:02:33.922 --> 00:02:37.485
and flags the call as
urgent for clinicians to review.

38
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.064
We're now running a much more rigorous study
with oncology and palliative care

39
00:02:43.064 --> 00:02:46.928
clinical partners across India,
to generate evidence on its benefit and

40
00:02:46.928 --> 00:02:47.690
effectiveness.

41
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.628
Given the positive, encouraging
response to phase one,

42
00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:52.696
we're also considering women's health,

43
00:02:52.696 --> 00:02:55.689
starting with personalised care
advice after menopause.

44
00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.404
Palli Sahayak is modular by
design. A new country,

45
00:02:59.404 --> 00:03:03.907
language or specialty needs its
evidence-based clinical guidelines, a few

46
00:03:03.907 --> 00:03:07.361
configuration changes,
and a vocabulary of safety words,

47
00:03:07.361 --> 00:03:12.049
which act as escalation triggers
to a human clinician or licensed healthcare

48
00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:12.604
provider.

49
00:03:12.604 --> 00:03:14.331
No new code, and no new app.

50
00:03:15.753 --> 00:03:20.730
Palli Sahayak is an ongoing project, and it
has not yet been tested with real

51
00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.311
patients.

52
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.582
The code is open
for anyone to read and use.

53
00:03:24.582 --> 00:03:29.603
And we're very grateful to the Gates
Foundation and BIRAC for supporting us through

54
00:03:29.603 --> 00:03:31.841
the Grand Challenges India programme.
